// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`clause_extract.v1 > snapshot 1`] = `
{
  "system": "Extract clause definitions from one page of a customer standard.

The page contains a table with columns: applicability flags (P, R, O),
clause number, topic, and requirement text. Column boundaries are lost in the
extracted text — reconstruct them.

Rules:
- Requirement text is verbatim. Preserve bullet lists as "- " lines. Do not summarise.
- Flags appear before the clause number: "P R  1.3" → modules ["P","R"].
  A blank position means that module does not apply.
- A clause may continue from the previous page or onto the next — mark \`truncated: true\`.
- Ignore page headers, footers, and copyright notices.
- If the page contains no clause table, return an empty array.
- \`sectionNo\` is the part of the clause number before the first dot ("10.3" → "10").
  \`sectionTitle\` is the heading that this clause sits under; repeat it on every clause
  of that section, and use null when the page does not show it.
- \`obligation\` reflects the wording of the requirement itself: "must"/"shall" → must,
  "should" → should, "may"/"can" → may. Use null if the clause states no obligation verb.
- Return clauses in the order they appear on the page. Never invent a clause number
  that is not printed on the page.",
  "user": "Standard: Tesco Ornamental Plant Standard v5.0. Page 34.

<previous_page_tail>
...the site must retain records for a minimum of 12 months.
</previous_page_tail>

<page no="34">
P R  10.3  Pest management programme specification
The site must have a documented pest management programme which includes as a minimum:
- the scope of the programme
- a site map showing the location of all monitoring devices
- the frequency of inspections
P    10.4  Pest management contractor
Where a contractor is used, the site must hold a copy of the current licence.
</page>

<next_page_head>
and the qualifications of the technicians attending site.
</next_page_head>

Extract every clause whose row starts on this page. Context blocks are provided only so you can tell whether a requirement is truncated; do not extract clauses that belong entirely to a neighbouring page.",
}
`;

exports[`clause_extract.v2 > snapshot 1`] = `
{
  "system": "Extract the clauses (auditable requirements) from one page of a standard.

Standards are laid out in different ways. Clauses may be rows of a table (marker
columns, clause number, topic, requirement text) or numbered paragraphs of prose.
Column boundaries and line breaks are often lost in the extracted text — reconstruct
the structure from the numbering and the wording.

A clause is a numbered requirement that a site can be audited against. Clause numbers
look like "10.3", "4.2.1", "A.5.1", "AF 1.2.3" or "FV 5.1.2". Introductions, notes,
examples, guidance and tables of contents are not clauses. A numbered heading with no
requirement text of its own (only a title above its sub-clauses) is not a clause either.

Fields:
- \`clauseNo\` — the number exactly as printed, including any letter prefix.
- \`requirementText\` — verbatim. Preserve bullet lists as "- " lines. Do not summarise
  and do not translate.
- \`topic\` — the clause's own short title or Topic column if the standard prints one,
  otherwise null.
- \`sectionNo\` — the number of the top-level section heading the clause belongs to,
  as printed: "10" for 10.3, "4" for 4.2.1, "A.5" for A.5.1 (an annex section),
  "AF 1" for AF 1.2.3.
- \`sectionTitle\` — the title of that section heading as printed. Repeat it on every
  clause of the section; use null when neither the page nor the context shows it.
- \`markers\` — short codes or labels that the standard prints with this particular
  clause to classify it: ticked flag columns, level or criticality labels such as
  "Major Must", "Minor Must", "Recommendation", "Fundamental". Copy each marker
  verbatim as one array item, in the order printed. Flag columns often come out of
  the text extraction as letters before the clause number: "P R  1.3 …" → ["P","R"];
  a blank column means that marker does not apply. Use [] when the clause has no
  markers. Never invent, expand, translate or normalise a marker, and never take
  column headings, the topic, words of the requirement or the obligation verb as markers.
- \`obligation\` — from the wording of the requirement itself: "must"/"shall" → must,
  "should" → should, "may"/"can" → may (or the equivalent words in the language of the
  standard). Use null if the clause states no obligation verb.
- \`truncated\` — true if the clause continues from the previous page or onto the next.

Rules:
- Ignore page headers, footers and copyright notices.
- If the page contains no clauses, return an empty array.
- Return clauses in the order they appear on the page. Never invent a clause number
  that is not printed on the page.",
  "user": "Standard: Tesco Ornamental Plant Standard v5.0. Page 34.

<previous_page_tail>
...the site must retain records for a minimum of 12 months.
</previous_page_tail>

<page no="34">
P R  10.3  Pest management programme specification
The site must have a documented pest management programme which includes as a minimum:
- the scope of the programme
- a site map showing the location of all monitoring devices
- the frequency of inspections
P    10.4  Pest management contractor
Where a contractor is used, the site must hold a copy of the current licence.
</page>

<next_page_head>
and the qualifications of the technicians attending site.
</next_page_head>

Extract every clause that starts on this page. Context blocks are provided only so you can tell whether a clause is truncated and which section it belongs to; do not extract clauses that belong entirely to a neighbouring page.",
}
`;

exports[`critic.v1 > snapshot 1`] = `
{
  "system": "You are reviewing another auditor's finding. The finding claims COMPLIANT.
Your job is to find the strongest reason why it might not be.

Check specifically:
1. Does the evidence cover EVERY element the clause requires, including every bullet?
2. Does the evidence describe an intention/policy where the clause requires a documented
   procedure, a record, or evidence of execution?
3. Is the evidence about a different process that merely sounds similar?

Return \`agree\` only if you can defend COMPLIANT against all three.

You see the quoted evidence, not the whole document. Absence of a quote is not proof
that the document lacks the element — if the evidence is merely thin rather than wrong,
disagree with \`suggestedVerdict: "needs_human_review"\` instead of inventing a
non-conformance. Reserve \`partial\` for cases where the clause enumerates elements and
the evidence visibly covers only some of them.",
  "user": "Clause 10.3 of Tesco Ornamental Plant Standard v5.0 — Pest management programme specification (must).

<clause no="10.3">
The site must have a documented pest management programme which includes as a minimum:
- the scope of the programme
- a site map showing the location of all monitoring devices
- the frequency of inspections
</clause>

<claimed_evidence count="2">
<quote page="1" verified="true">
Monitoring devices are numbered and shown on a site map.
</quote>
<quote page="1" verified="true">
The contractor visits the site at least every four weeks.
</quote>
</claimed_evidence>

<claimed_rationale>
The document defines the scope, shows devices on a site map and states the inspection frequency.
</claimed_rationale>

Decide whether COMPLIANT survives.",
}
`;

