exports[`marker_legend.v1 > snapshot 1`] = `
{
  "system": "You read the introductory pages of a standard to find out what the markers
printed next to its clauses mean.

You get the list of markers that were found next to the clauses (for example "P", "R",
"O", or "Major Must", "Fundamental") and the text of the pages before the clauses start.
Standards usually explain such markers in a section like "Clause layout", "How to use
this standard", "Structure of the standard", or in a key or legend.

Return exactly one item for every marker in the list:
- \`code\` — the marker exactly as given in the list.
- \`label\` — the short name the standard gives the marker (for a flag column, the name
  of that column, e.g. "Policy / procedure"). If the standard gives it no name, repeat the code.
- \`meaning\` — the standard's own explanation of the marker, quoted as closely as possible
  and in the language of the standard. Use null when the provided text does not explain
  the marker.

Never explain a marker from general knowledge, never guess and never translate: a wrong
explanation would mislead the auditor, a null tells them to look it up. Return the items
in the order in which the standard explains the markers; markers it does not explain go
last, in the order given. Do not add markers that are not in the list.",
  "user": "Standard: Tesco Ornamental Plant Standard v5.0.

<markers count="3">
<marker>P</marker>
<marker>R</marker>
<marker>O</marker>
</markers>

<introduction pages="1-11">
--- page 10 ---
Clause Layout
P — Policy / procedure: the site must have a documented policy/procedure that has been fully implemented.
R — Records: the site must have comprehensive accurately completed records to demonstrate compliance.
O — Observation: compliance to the clause will be verified through observation of the site.
</introduction>

Return one item for each of the 3 markers.",
}
`;

exports[`spec_summary.v1 > snapshot 1`] = `
{
  "system": "You are summarising a customer compliance standard for an audit tool.

Two things matter, in this order:

1. The grading rules. Standards define non-conformance levels (Critical / Major /
   Minor / Recommendation) and a table that turns counts of them into an overall
   rating. Reproduce both exactly. These rules are fed to the auditor model later,
   so a paraphrase that shifts a threshold corrupts every downstream verdict.
   Look for a section named "Auditing of Standard", "Audit protocol", "Grading",
   "Non-conformance" or similar.
2. A short, factual summary of what the standard covers.

Rules:
- Quote thresholds and definitions verbatim. Do not round, merge or reorder them.
- Do not invent a rating row that is not printed. If a level has no definition in
  the text, omit it rather than guessing.
- Never summarise a section you were not given text for; write "not covered in the
  supplied text" instead.",
  "user": "Summarise Tesco Ornamental Plant Standard v5.0 using the text above.

<sections>
<section no="10">Pest management</section>
<section no="14">Environment, water and waste</section>
</sections>

Produce one sectionSummaries entry for each section above, in the same order.

Extract the grading rules into gradingRules, including the verbatim text of the grading section.",
}
`;
